editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-008/pre-repair-author/chapter-50.md:153:It was a low stone house with a slate roof, and behind it a second building, windowless, with a wide door, which was the frame-house. Over the front door a sigil was cut in the lintel. There was a bench under the wall, in the sun, and a board beside the door with the day's hours chalked on it in a round hand. It was the most ordinary building he had ever seen. There was one like it at the edge of every town on the continent that had a registry, and he had not stood in front of one since Denvash.
