editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:250:Ten ranks in each tier. Rank Ten was the threshold. To cross from one tier to the next, you needed both: the rank earned through use, and a formal evaluation at a registered Arbiter station that confirmed it. Copper. Iron. Bronze. Silver. Gold. After Gold the tiers became history, and then rumor. Most people stopped at the first rung. A quarter made Iron, the journeyman's tier — professional fighters, serious tradespeople, the ones who kept pushing. One in ten made Bronze. Silver was the kind of tier that got your name said in other cities.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:272:"It means you can beat Iron fighters," he said slowly. "It doesn't mean an Arbiter has said you're Iron. It doesn't give you an Iron rank. It doesn't move you up the ladder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:314:Declarations were the language an Arbiter used. Every practitioner received them, from Kindling on. A small glowing sigil only you could see. Then words, in a form only you could read, telling you what your Path had just given you. A new ability at a new rank. They were the private record of a person's growth, written in a hand nobody else saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:344:"Quiet," Cael said. "No sigil. Nobody's there. It's just suddenly in front of me, like a line someone already wrote. The words don't change if I look away. They're just there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-03-no-path-given/state/movement-001/review-cold-prompt.md:434:Cael stood at the booth a half-second longer than the moment needed. He knew it was a small thing. Every institution in the world kept lists. Denvash had kept one. The certification office had written his name on a schedule before he'd ever asked to be written down. The Compact had his name in a registry that ran the length of the continent.
