editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:1732:# Chapter 16 — The Column
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:1892:"I've been a file since Denvash."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:1988:## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:1990:# Chapter 17 — Under the Gallery
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2215:Halfway along the second row a correspondent laid his board on the bench beside him and folded his arms and shut his eyes. At the back, a woman in a market shawl took out a half-knitted stocking. Two clerks from the river guild were playing some game on their knees with a stub of pencil and a scrap of paper. Nobody was angry. Nobody had stopped believing anything. They had come down the market street to see a case and somebody was reading them an inventory, and by the fortieth minute a third of the room had quietly gone somewhere else behind its own eyes. For nine minutes together not one pencil moved at the front benches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2235:## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2237:# Chapter 18 — Fifteen Words
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2465:## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2467:# Chapter 19 — The Shape
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2535:Every porter on the hill knew that hand by now. For two years letters in it had come up from Denvash, the address laid out in a square block as level as the bubble in a good spirit glass, and the post room had long since begun putting them on top. Cael took it out onto the covered walk and opened it as he went. He opened Hesk's letters walking and no others. In two years there had never been bad news in one of them, and the habit had grown up on that record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2547:*Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2559:"Denvash," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2565:Karis opened her notebook at the empty line, and wrote on it. *Denvash. Provincial registry. Trade licences and the title to the shop.* She put the date beside it. Then she laid her rule under all five lines and drew a stroke beneath them from one edge of the page to the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2577:"In the office we used to call it walking the fences." Seln turned his cup. "On paper it's a pre-restriction inventory."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2585:"Before the ruling." Seln turned the cup again. "Hold on to that. Nobody walks the fences of a field they expect to leave open. It would waste a clerk, and offices hate to waste clerks, so the inventory only ever starts once somebody, somewhere above the seat, has already settled in his own mind how the seat will rule. It's a finding in its own right. It just isn't made by anyone who sits in the seat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2597:"And then nothing at all." Seln took his finger back. "You won't find this part in any code. No office has ever needed it written down. A finished inventory isn't an instrument. Nobody serves it. Nobody publishes it. It has no title. It is five files in five drawers in five offices, every one of them lawful, every one as dull as a laundry list, and all five indexed under one name." He squared the four beans again with the side of his finger, and did not touch the fifth. "If the seat rules for the house, the five drawers are never opened together. In time each office weeds its old papers, as offices do, and not one person anywhere will have broken a single rule. If the seat rules the other way, it takes one clerk one afternoon to fetch the five files to one desk. Then every gate shuts on the same morning. And the enrollee finds out what that sixth week was for on a morning when there is nothing left to do about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2615:"We keep counting what they do," she said. She said it evenly, the way she read the true sum off a bout sheet after a loss. "And we're good. We're very good at the sittings. Karis's filing is the best thing anybody's ever written on this hill, and the house's counsel wins every point there is, and the seat writes down that she won it." She put the bean back exactly where it had been. "They'll let us be good at the sittings all year. It doesn't cost them a thing. Meanwhile there are men in Denvash asking an old man about a tin box." She looked round at each of them, one at a time, and her chin came up. "The question is what we do the day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2651:"And the inventory."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/review-editorial.stdout.log:2687:*And two polite men have been to Denvash to ask an old man whether my name is in a tin box under his bench.*
