editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2184:-So he had gone to the start. A woman at Denvash with a brass plate on her desk that sat dark for eleven seconds before it lit; a name stamped on a card; a fourteen-year-old put out of the back door with nothing written down anywhere that said she had been wrong. Three years to get one sentence on paper that undid her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2185:+"Denvash," he had said, and stopped, because that was enough. Every one of them knew the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2241: Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2496:+He had grown up across the lane from a carrier's yard at Denvash, and he knew what a horse does when a rider is about to mount: it feels the weight come before the rider's foot does, and shifts to take it, a little, without being asked. Every horse in that court had done it for its rider. Hers had not moved. It had stood like a post while she went up into the saddle, and had only felt her when she was already sitting on it. Nobody had told the horse she was coming, because her body had not said so. Somebody, a long time ago, had taken that warning out of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2644:    19	"Properly." Lira turned her hand over on her knee, which was the gesture she used when she had done more work than she intended to be praised for. "Three carters at the ferry landing who run the east road for a living, one at a time, on three different days, about three different things, because a woman asking about the same road three times in one week is a woman a carter remembers. It's four days to the Line from Ostrand with a wagon and three without. Six waystations, five of them registry-provisioned, the sixth private and past the last station. The Line isn't a line — there's no fence, no post, nothing. It's where the last Arbiter station is, and then there isn't another one, and the carters know exactly where it stops because that's where their manifests stop being checked." She let that sit for a second. "Two of them go past it. They said the same thing in different words: nobody out there asks what tier you are, because there's nobody to ask and nothing to check it against."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2656:    31	"Every institution we have ever been inside failed us until text held it accountable. Denvash put him out a back door with no record. Ardenmere's circuit rated him because Vell kept a ledger. Greyvane's hearing found for him because the clause was in a compilation nobody had read. Halcenvane enrolled him because the record was true and Bracken wouldn't falsify it. Norhold fielded him on a sentence." She counted them on her fingers, the way she counted precedents. "Text is the only thing that has ever held. Not once — *every time.*" She set her hand flat on the face-down papers. "And the record of walking out mid-proceedings reads *fled.* It's the registry's word. I've read it in two hundred files. *Respondent fled the jurisdiction pending ruling* — and after it, in every file, the ruling goes in anyway, by default, and the default is the petition's whole prayer, granted, with a line beneath it that says the respondent's flight is *itself evidence of the integrity question.*" She sat down. "Leave, and we hand them the confession they couldn't get from Umber, from Vastin, from Daeva, from eleven pounds of the truth. That's the record. I'm the archive. I'm telling you what the archive says."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2708:    83	"Denvash," Cael said. "A man in an office decided what I was in eleven seconds and there was no text in the room, and I was fourteen, and it took me three years and four institutions to get a sentence written down that undid it. Every good thing in my life since has been text somebody wrote down and then had to honor." He looked at Lira, whose question it was. "The system has never beaten me when the text was in the room. I'm not leaving the room while the text still works."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2751:   126	Four registers of not-answering, he had counted at the last council. Tonight there were five, and the fifth was different in kind. Karis's silences were the pause before a citation. Brom's were weight. Lira's were the half-second in which she decided how much of a true thing a room could carry. Cael's own, he knew perfectly well, were inventory.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:2769:   144	And a locked case on a shelf in his quarters that had outlasted every one of the nineteen. It had traveled to Norhold and back unopened. It had ridden in a cart under the same canvas as three boxes of a researcher's compiled truth without either archive knowing the other's contents, and he had sat on the cart's bench for two days beside those boxes and had thought about the symmetry for most of the second day.
