editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3613:    69	He told her exactly. That surprised her. He knew the last station by its shape, a low stone building with a frame-house behind it and a sigil cut over the door. He knew how far it stood past the last chartered waystation, and which side of the road, and what the ground looked like after it, which was the same as the ground before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3866:    49	"Denvash," he had said, and stopped, because that was enough. Every one of them knew the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3896:    79	Cael did not need to be told what was under the hand. A square cream envelope with a dark red seal on it, unbroken, which had come up the hill twenty-three days ago and had not been opened since. Under it, the summons, the docket, the district's confirmation with *outcome anticipated* on it in a clerk's round hand, and a sitting on the last Fifth-day of the year at which three assessors and a sigil frame would almost certainly find Brom an Iron. His family's ledger had been waiting for that line since before he could lift a practice weight. In the second box was a grandmother at the head of a long table, and a word he had never once said in the same breath as *east*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:3958:   141	He had packed his own box nineteen times. That was the thing nobody at the table knew about him, and it had come into his head while the big one was naming his shirts. A posting ended on a morning like any other; a letter came; a man put what he owned into one box and his papers into another and went down to the coach. He knew to the ounce what a life weighed when it had to be carried. He knew, too, that the box you packed was never the problem. It was the other one, the one that would not go in a box at all, and he had watched the big one put his hand flat over it and say so, and nobody at the table had asked, and Seln had liked them all very much for that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:4098:-So he had gone to the start. A woman at Denvash with a brass plate on her desk that sat dark for eleven seconds before it lit; a name stamped on a card; a fourteen-year-old put out of the back door with nothing written down anywhere that said she had been wrong. Three years to get one sentence on paper that undid her.
