editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:5304:   103	They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:5523:    81	He had grown up across the lane from a carrier's yard at Denvash, and he knew what a horse does when a rider is about to mount: it feels the weight come before the rider's foot does, and shifts to take it, a little, without being asked. Every horse in that court had done it for its rider. Hers had not moved. It had stood like a post while she went up into the saddle, and had only felt her when she was already sitting on it. Nobody had told the horse she was coming, because her body had not said so. Somebody, a long time ago, had taken that warning out of her.
