editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7586:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md-169-He found he was not frightened. Not yet. He was something nearer cold. It was the feeling he had had on the morning of his Kindling at Denvash, standing in a frame that had nothing to say about him and was perfectly calm about it. The fright would come later, he knew, at the window, in the dark, where there was time for it; not here, where there was work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8000:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:5:Vastin held the petition up to the window and tilted it until the light lay along the wax. The Archmarshal's mark was all there, and the ring of small letters round it, but its left edge was shallow, almost a ghost, where the press had bitten at a slant. It was the press's fault. He had screwed it to the corner of the desk himself on his first morning in this room, nineteen years ago, in a hurry and out of true, and had never straightened it. In nineteen years it had never mattered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8225:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Every person who has ever decided a thing about me had a face, and I could read it. Pellin at Denvash, with her brass plate. Coss across a desk. Vastin with his frames. The provost who was frightened and couldn't hide it. Umber polishing his spectacles over a sheet he couldn't score. I read every one of them from across a room, and every reading was worth something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-004/recheck-r2.stdout.log:8235:editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md-225-Cael noticed it before he noticed anything else about the second sitting, because he had been watching the benches instead of the clerk for some while by then. Halfway along the second row a correspondent had laid his board on the bench beside him and folded his arms and shut his eyes. At the back a woman in a market shawl had got out a half-knitted stocking. Two clerks from the river guild were playing some game on their knees with a stub of pencil and a scrap of paper. Nobody was angry and nobody had stopped believing anything. They had come down the market street to see a case and somebody was reading them an inventory. At the front benches not one pencil had moved for nine minutes.
