editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-44.md:1:# Chapter 44 — Standing Room
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-44.md:75:It was a Force against a quick lad of nineteen or twenty whose Path Reydan could not name from the rope, something light and fast that wanted distance. The lad was good. He kept the Force at the end of his reach for two exchanges, darting in and away. The room liked him; there were girls on the second bench who shouted his name. In the third he came in once too often on the same line, half a step deeper than he had come before. The Force had been building all that time, slow and deep, and had been waiting for exactly that half-step. He put the whole of what he had built into the lad's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-44.md:87:His father's house had a training yard, because his father was a guild officer of the middling sort who believed in such things, and at ten Reydan had been put in it with a hired man who was paid to make him better. At eleven, when he Kindled with a Pressure Path that the Arbiter wrote down as burst-compression and the examiners called promising, the academy took him. He had been there, in one way or another, ever since. He had risen through every examination his tier asked of him, cleanly and on time. He had sparred every morning of his life against people who were paid to find what was wrong with him and say so, and he had been told what was wrong with him so many times, and so precisely, that at twenty-two there was very little left that anybody could find.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-007/pre-repair/chapter-44.md:91:The guild bouts had stopped meaning anything around his nineteenth year. It had not happened all at once. Opponents had begun to be ill on the days they were drawn against him. Trainers had begun to speak to their prospects quietly before a draw went up. Then, three autumns running, the two academies in his city had redrawn their invitational so that he and the other house's best boy sat in brackets that could not meet before the final. On the third autumn the other house withdrew its boy before the final, with a letter about an injured wrist. Everybody knew the wrist was well. Nobody said so.
