editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:2665:# Chapter 21 — Off Side
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:2951:"There's a page in a hall in Denvash." Cael looked along the row at the lamp in the boarding-house window, because it was easier than looking up. "It's got my name on it, and the day, and one word, and the word isn't a Path. They didn't have a row to put me in. So they wrote down the nearest thing to nothing they had, and shut the book, and the next boy came up." He put his hands under his arms. "That was them deciding. It took about as long as it takes to say it. I'd rather take a year and get it right, from what I can actually see, than walk about wearing their word because they were quicker."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:2977:## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:2979:# Chapter 22 — The Shelf
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:3219:He thought of the brown register in the reading room over the dyers' hall, a hundred years old, with a row in it for every Path that anybody had ever been recorded holding, and no row for anybody who had not been given one. He thought of a hall in Denvash, and a clerk's flat voice, and a word written down because there was nothing else to write. For a year he had believed that the trouble was the books. The books were old, and kept by people who owned the key, and had been built in rows by people who had only ever seen what fitted in a row. Now here was a man who had built his own book out of nothing but standing close to people, without a registry or an almanac or a guild, out of his own body, honestly, for two years. And his book had no shelf for him either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/review-cold.stdout.log:3285:"Who stood on her heels for two hours being a man she'd never fought, so you'd stop reaching for your hip?" She did not wait. "Whose dock partner showed you the gap? Who writes to you from Denvash every month to tell you to eat? Whose book do you read your own name in every morning, that's just this week written down that she was wrong, so you'd know the rest of it's right?" She turned and looked at him. "And who sat on a bench across from you for twelve days, letting you count him, and then told you what he saw when you asked him, when he didn't have to and it didn't win him anything?"
