editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:5979:editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-21.md:287:"There's a page in a hall in Denvash." Cael looked along the row at the lamp in the boarding-house window, because it was easier than looking up. "It's got my name on it, and the day, and one word, and the word isn't a Path. They didn't have a row to put me in. So they wrote down the nearest thing to nothing they had, and shut the book, and the next boy came up." He put his hands under his arms. "That was them deciding. It took about as long as it takes to say it. I'd rather take a year and get it right, from what I can actually see, than walk about wearing their word because they were quicker."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6172:editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:He thought of the brown register in the reading room over the dyers' hall, a hundred years old, with a row in it for every Path that anybody had ever been recorded holding, and no row for anybody who had not been given one. He thought of a hall in Denvash, and a clerk's flat voice, and a word written down because there was nothing else to write. For a year he had believed that the trouble was the books. The books were old, and kept by people who owned the key, and had been built in rows by people who had only ever seen what fitted in a row. Now here was a man who had built his own book out of nothing but standing close to people, without a registry or an almanac or a guild, out of his own body, honestly, for two years. And his book had no shelf for him either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6174:editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-22.md:303:"Who stood on her heels for two hours being a man she'd never fought, so you'd stop reaching for your hip?" She did not wait. "Whose dock partner showed you the gap? Who writes to you from Denvash every month to tell you to eat? Whose book do you read your own name in every morning, that's just this week written down that she was wrong, so you'd know the rest of it's right?" She turned and looked at him. "And who sat on a bench across from you for twelve days, letting you count him, and then told you what he saw when you asked him, when he didn't have to and it didn't win him anything?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6194:editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:Then he folded it and put it with the other, unfinished one on the shelf, and lay down. It would reach Denvash in about a week. Whatever happened on the main floor would have happened by then, and Hesk would read the letter at the kitchen table knowing nothing of it, in the twenty minutes of sideways light, and Cael would be the only one of them who knew how it had come out. He thought that there was something fair in that, though he could not have said what, and on the thought he slept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6208:editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-16.md:113:He had stood in the station's bare room under the Arbiter with his mother's hand on his shoulder, and heard it read out in the clerk's flat voice. Iron Skin was a Path filed among the defensive ones, the kind that kept you whole. He was Copper, the first metal, with ten ranks to climb before anybody would look at him again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6233:# Chapter 8 — Brom
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6333:The two-week problem had a second dimension, and he made himself write that one down too, because leaving it unwritten wouldn't make it smaller. His own inventory, as it stood tonight: the Wind-adjacent fragment, fluent now within its terms — the seam guarded by footwork, the landing beat priced and repurposed, three bursts an exchange before the hip and the air gave out. The Pressure-adjacent fragment, dependable and slow — a full breath to gather, a visible sink in his stance, one limb's worth of dispersal, the upper limit still an unopened box. Against most opponents, the inventory plus the watching was enough; the year's record said so. Against a defensive specialist whose entire Path was built to receive force — against a man whose surface could apparently be *hard where and when he chose* — he could not currently name what either fragment bought him. Evasion won nothing from an opponent who didn't need to hit you to outlast you. Dispersal won nothing from an opponent who dealt no force to disperse until he chose to. Two weeks, he wrote, to learn what the locked door was actually protecting. The written record first — due diligence, even knowing how little of it there will be — then the man himself. He knows I'll be watching. He's chosen to be watchable. Add that to the file too — an opponent who wants to be studied is telling you something about what he thinks study will find.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6369:He read the last clause back and let it stand, because the honesty in it was the kind he'd built both notebooks to protect. He expected to lose. The inventory said so; the locked door said so; the calm arithmetic he ran on every scheduled fight said so, and he'd never once improved a fight's odds by lying to the page about them. What the arithmetic couldn't price — what he noticed in himself with the detached interest he'd once reserved for other people's anomalies — was that the expected loss changed nothing about the anticipation. Somewhere in a year of Ardenmere, losing to the right opponent had stopped being a cost and become a purchase. Feryn had taught him that. He suspected, closing the notebook and turning down the lamp, that Brom was about to teach him what the next price bracket looked like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6376:# Chapter 9 — Preparation
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6531:# Chapter 10 — The Observation Principle
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6551:Cael's last notation of the bout, written as the crowd noise rose around him, was not about Wendel at all: *She fights the fight in front of her. He fought the fight he trained for. That's the entire margin, and no Arbiter has ever had a field for it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-003/recheck-r1.stdout.log:6603:It was unglamorous work, and he was aware, chalk dust on his hands, of how it would have looked to anyone from his old life in Denvash — a boy alone in a stone alcove, running between marks, hitting a post, sitting down to write about it. It looked like nothing. It was, he had come to believe, most of everything. The fragments had arrived on their own terms, unasked for, unexplained. What they became was decided here, in the counting.
