editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2322:# Chapter 11 — Pressed
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2575:# Chapter 12 — Six Phases
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2791:He thought about the word the registry had given him in a hall at fourteen, with the Arbiter gone dark over his head after eleven seconds. Somebody had done a sum about him too, in front of everybody, and written the answer down, and he had never once thought of answering it. He had only been getting on with things, a page at a time, in three columns, in the dark. Now, sitting beside her on the step, he wondered whether that was the same thing she meant, done more quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2802:# Chapter 13 — The Wrong Things
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:2924:He did it at the crate desk with the door between the rooms open and the light from the yard window coming in grey. He began with the oldest notebook on the shelf, the one he had carried out of Denvash, written in the round anxious hand he had had at fourteen, and then came forward through the year to the grey book on his desk. He was trying to read them not as the boy who had written them, who knew what every line meant because he had been there, but as the big man had read them: as a stranger on a bench, from the side, a hand's breadth away, seeing only what was on the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:3019:# Chapter 14 — Pace
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:3023:That was what the Ironyard called him, the young Blade from the salt end, and nobody used his name, though everybody knew it. He was nineteen and quick and Iron-equivalent in Vell's book since the spring, and he won by owning the time. He came out at the word and set a rhythm, a beat of his own, and made whoever was across from him fight to it, and by the third exchange they were dancing to his count without knowing it, and by the fourth he was hitting them on the beats they had not known were coming. The betting man would not take money on how long his bouts lasted, because they lasted exactly as long as the clock wanted them to.
