editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:9276:books/book-01-the-shattered/chapters/chapter-10.md:25:He stood in the dark courtyard for a long time after that, not trying anymore, just standing, letting the cold settle into his hands and a quiet dread settle into a place his usual detachment couldn't quite reach. He thought about Lira's easy description of checking her Arbiter — *like checking a ledger* — and about how casually she'd said it, the way you describe something so ordinary you've forgotten it's a privilege. He thought about the four names in the historical archive, about Hesk's careful non-answers, about the marked circle in the certification office and the eleven seconds that had started all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:9279:books/book-01-the-shattered/chapters/chapter-10.md:127:They went through the combination again, and again, until his feet stopped needing his permission to move correctly, and there was something genuinely useful in that — not the technique itself, though the technique was useful too, but the absorbing focus the drilling demanded, a kind of thinking that left no room for anything else. Somewhere around the twentieth repetition, sweat starting to sting his eyes, he stopped noticing the counting entirely, which was, he understood only afterward, the actual point of the exercise — not the footwork itself but the mercy of a task that asked for his whole attention and gave him, in exchange, an hour where the Arbiter and the courtyard and the four names in the archive simply weren't the loudest thing in the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:9338:books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-19.md:111:Prynn stood in the archive doorway for all of it, requiring — and getting — a signed inventory line for every box that crossed her threshold, her silence more expensive to be near than most people's shouting. Karis watched from beside her, the copy of the passage folded twice inside her coat, close enough to her chest that Cael, standing on her other side, understood without being told exactly how much weight she'd assigned to keeping it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-02-iron-circuit/state/movement-002/recheck-r1.stdout.log:9344:books/book-03-no-path-given/chapters/chapter-20.md:25:"The flag is Arbiter output. It's not a classification. Classification is created by the enumerated schedule — every Path, every tier, every provisional and disputed category the Compact has ever claimed authority over, all of it named, all of it written down. [SHATTERED] isn't in the schedule. It's never been in the schedule. Four hundred years of enforcement custom built on a word the law itself never defined." He said it the way he'd said it a hundred times in the archive, alone, to Prynn, to the empty shelves — and it still landed differently out loud, in front of people, with Brom's borrowed flatness staring back at him like the real thing. "You can't expel me under rules that don't contain me."
