     1	# Chapter 21 — Resignations
     2	
     3	The walk-out took a morning and an afternoon, and its whole discipline was smallness: no speeches, no crowd at the gate, no institutional convulsion. Paperwork, boxes, and doors, in the order the day actually ran them, because the order was the dignity.
     4	
     5	---
     6	
     7	Morning: Bracken's counter.
     8	
     9	The records office opened at the first bell as it opened every day of the year, and the four of them were the first business of it, and Bracken had known they would be, and had set out four leaves of the correct form the night before, squared, with the inkstand where the light was good and a second pen laid beside the first in case a nib went. Cael counted the counter in on the way through the door because he had counted it in on his first morning at Halcenvane and intended to be consistent about it: the long oak top worn pale at the two places where hands rested; the pigeonholes behind, forty of them, alphabetical; the completed tray at the left and the pending at the right and the standing rule, posted in Bracken's hand for thirty years, that nothing crossed from right to left unverified. The morning light came in from the east window and lay along the counter in a bar, and the bar reached the completed tray at about the second bell, and Cael had watched it reach that tray perhaps two hundred times without ever once thinking he would remember it.
    10	
    11	The form was Registry Form of Withdrawal from Enrollment, four clauses and a signature block, the same leaf a first-year filled in when a family called them home. It did not have a clause for anything that was actually happening.
    12	
    13	They filed in the order they presented, and they presented in an order nobody had discussed and everybody understood. Lira first, because Lira went first at things. She read the four clauses through, which she did now with everything on paper and had not done at fourteen, and signed in the hard fast hand of a woman who had learned to write her own name on a form as an act rather than a formality. Bracken initialed the leaf, entered the date, entered the time, and moved it left.
    14	
    15	Brom second. He read it twice, the second time with a finger under the third clause, which surrendered the enrollee's claim to the institution's remaining obligations for the term. "That's fees," Bracken said, without being asked. "You're four days short of term. The academy owes you four days of instruction it will not be providing. The clause says you're not asking for it back." Brom considered that for the length of time Brom considered things and then said, "Fine," and signed, and Cael, watching, understood that the pause had not been about money and that Brom had wanted the sentence explained out loud so he would know precisely what he was giving up, all of it, including the four days.
    16	
    17	Cael third. He had drafted his the night before and copied it fair on the counter at dawn, and the copying had taken a quarter of an hour for four clauses because he had found himself reading each one entirely, the way you read a thing you are never going to be inside again. He signed, and Bracken initialed, and there it was: the demonstration-provision enrollment, the one the whole continent had spent a term arguing about, closed by the four-clause form a homesick first-year uses.
    18	
    19	Bracken put the same question to each of them before the pen went down, because the code obliged him to and because he had never in thirty years abbreviated an obligation: whether the withdrawal was made freely, without compulsion by any officer of the institution. Lira said, "Freely." Brom said, "Yes." Cael said, "Freely," and then, because the man deserved the whole of it and because the leaf would sit in a district file for forty years, added, "and not by any officer of this institution, which I would like entered as written and not summarized."
    20	
    21	Bracken looked at him for a moment over the counter.
    22	
    23	"It will be entered as you said it," he said, and wrote it out longhand in the margin, which the form did not provide for and which no auditor would ever query, because a registrar's marginal notation in his own hand carries the weight of the registrar, and this registrar's weight was thirty years.
    24	
    25	Seln did not appear at the counter, because Seln was staff, and staff did not withdraw from enrollment, and there was no leaf on the counter for what Seln was going to do. His absence from the sequence was noted by exactly two people in the room and neither of them said a word about it.
    26	
    27	Karis went last, and Karis's was not four clauses.
    28	
    29	---
    30	
    31	The forfeiture ran to a second form and a schedule of line items, and Bracken read every one of them aloud, because the code required the forfeiting party to hear what was being surrendered, spoken, in the presence of the registrar, before the hand went to the page.
    32	
    33	He read them in the schedule's own order and he read them at counter pace, neither hurrying to spare her nor slowing to make a moment of it, which was the only mercy his office had available and which he spent completely.
    34	
    35	"Tier certification, Iron, Rank Three, issued under the district seal. Retained." He looked up. "That one you keep. Certification is not enrollment. Nobody can take a tier from you; they can only decline to count it."
    36	
    37	"I know," Karis said. "Read the rest."
    38	
    39	"Research standing, Halcenvane Academy, granted on faculty recommendation. Forfeit." He entered it. "Research allocation, current term, unexpended balance. Forfeit and returned to the general account. Courier privileges under the academy seal. Forfeit. Archive privileges, general collection. Forfeit. Archive privileges, restricted collection, granted by chancellor's warrant. Forfeit." A short pause while his pen caught up with his voice. "Annex allocation, north room, with lock and key. Surrendered; key to be returned at this counter. Standing request in progress with Greyvane archive under academy budget code, unfilled. Cancelled at the seal, with the requesting institution's regrets. Reading-room precedence. Forfeit. Faculty dining entitlement." He said that one exactly as flatly as the others. "Forfeit. Assistant, second-year, assigned by faculty recommendation. Reassigned, effective this date, to the general instruction pool."
    40	
    41	He set the pen down. "That is the schedule entire. The code obliges me to ask whether you have heard each item read and whether you understand that the forfeiture is not reversible by application, only by fresh grant."
    42	
