   162	
   163	The road stopped being a road at about the third hour — not dramatically; it simply stopped being maintained, and then stopped being surfaced, and then became the track that two carts a season make, and Brom got down and walked at the horse's head for the last of it. There were no milestones. There was no post. Twice they passed cut wood stacked by the track, which meant somebody within a day's walk was in the business of cutting it, and once, at a distance, a fenced enclosure with sheep in it and nobody visible.
