    46	
    47	*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — Feryn. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — source protected, sealed still, the logic updated and the discipline held: a defector's tradecraft in my hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition, so the seal stays, and Seln knows now that it does, and knows why, which changes nothing about the seal and everything about the debt. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; the piece of the machine itself. Blade-adjacent — Ephram; the only "non-hostile" in the log; taken in earnest from a friend who asked to be shown straight.*
    48	
    49	*Nine integrated abilities. Each with a name beside it, one of them protected and one of them missing — and the missing line I have left blank on purpose, because the blank is the truest thing the entry says.*
    50	
    51	*One anomaly. Session nine. Tide-adjacent. Turned over once, per the custom at intervals that matter — checked against the cache, checked against everything the cache explained, and touching none of it. Still open. Still real. Patience.*
    52	
    53	He read the page back before he sanded it, which was also the custom, and found the two things the page did not say and would not say, and let it not say them.
    54	
    55	The first was that the nine had cost. Not in the entry's ledger sense — the costs were charted elsewhere, hourly, in the notebook: the pulls, the four weeks of an architecture reseating around Anchor-adjacent's fixed-point grammar and then, on top of it and before it had finished, the edge-lines and the line-reading of a friend's Blade doctrine arriving to be housed. The forearm from the Crown yard was a week old and had gone the colour of a bad plum and would be gone in another week. The bill for eleven minutes at a rate the managed version had never had to hold had come due on the second day of the road, all at once, as a headache and an inability to eat, and had been paid, and the log had said so at the time.
    56	
    57	The second was that the entry could be read, by a stranger, as a list of nine victories. It was a list of nine people. Six of them had given him something without knowing, at the time, that they had; one had asked to be shown straight and had been, and had given it anyway; one had wanted to end him and now wanted an answer instead; and one had no name, and would never have one, and had been sent to hold him by an office that had not troubled to record which of its instruments it was spending.
    58	
    59	The anomaly he turned over the way he turned it over at every interval that mattered, which was: he opened the session-nine page, read his own fourteen-year-old handwriting, checked it against everything that had been added to the world since he last checked it, and closed it again.
    60	
    61	It was a shorter procedure this year than any year before it, and the shortness was itself the finding. He had spent five months in the company of an archive that explained two hundred years of the machine's private business — a modification, a corrections function, a contingency that fired when the corrections function could not — and he had laid the anomaly beside all of it in an annex, deliberately, expecting a fit, and had found none. Not a partial fit. Not a suggestive one. The cache explained a great many things and did not touch this, and he had written the sentence at the time and would not improve on it now.
    62	
