   291	In another place, that week, a file was closed.
   292	
   293	Nothing can be said of the room. It may be said that there was a table, and a lamp, since the work could not have been done without them. There was a clerk, and of the clerk it can be said only that the hand was practised, and that it had closed a great many files.
   294	
   295	This one had the name on its cover that the instruments used. It was thick; it ran from a single leaf, taken down in a district office at the far end of the country, to four lines sent up that week from a seat at Ostrand. The clerk had those four lines on a slip and was to enter them at the file's foot, in order.
   296	
   297	*Enrollment: closed by resignation.*
   298	
   299	*Proceedings: moot.*
   300	
   301	*Re-evaluation: not executed.*
   302	
   303	*Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
   304	
   305	The clerk wrote them. Each was copied as it stood, and each was true. A file with those four lines at its foot was a finished thing, and it would ordinarily have been tied, and stamped, and carried down to the press where finished things were kept.
   306	
   307	There was a second slip.
   308	
   309	It lay in the pocket inside the file's front cover, where slips of advice were put. The clerk had not put it there. It carried no office's mark, and no initial, and it was in a plain copying hand that might have been anybody's.
   310	
