   364	
   365	He had been read at fourteen, in a room, in eleven seconds, and since that morning there had always been somebody deciding what he was. A warden had done it, and a provost, and a tournament office; a seat had done it under a hood, and a designation had done it that no office would own. Some of them had been kind, and most had been correct, and all of them had been writing in a column that he could not see.
   366	
   367	He listened for it now, on a shelf of rock, as he had listened at a ring of churned turf for a threshold that was not there.
   368	
   369	There was the fire, and the water. Four people were breathing.
