editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:3:The house kept no rest day in its last week, and the closing seminars sat through the Seventh-day as they did every year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:99:The queue was nobody, and the desk clerk's stool was empty, for the desk clerk kept his Seventh-days. Behind the counter, at the copying table under the long window, the teaching assistant sat with a quire of the wing's forms and copied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:107:"Then the wing receives it." He put out a hand, and took the card, and read both sides, and laid it in a tray. "The provision's hours were complete on the Fourth-day of the week before last. The card agrees. It is pleasant when they agree."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:111:Cael had been pushed one of those across this counter every week for two years, always in the desk clerk's hand. *Fifth-day, wing three. Supervised, per provision.* This one Seln wrote himself, in the small square hand of his margins, and he wrote one line on it and turned it round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:113:*First-day. The crossroads past the eighteenth stone, east. By dusk.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:223:"It's a good road. I like that hill, it's got a view." The officer tightened his girth, grunting. "I've had worse days than finding nobody to give a thing to. There's a frame-house booked for the Fourth-day, and I shall have to open it up regardless, for the book's sake, and stand about." He looked at Cael then, quite kindly, as he had looked at a girl of fourteen. "I'd have held the door for you. I want you to know it would have been done properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:309:It was six switchbacks. He had climbed them first with a provision and a stamp, and he had gone down them all this year in a coach, on the seat's Fifth-days, sitting up straight; and he walked them now beside a cart's wheel, with his hip complaining at each turn, where it went downhill, and his shoulder beginning to tell him about six pushes. He let them talk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:339:*Seventh-day. Four leaves at Bracken's counter, in the order we came: Lira, Brom, me, Karis. Entered and blotted. Nothing left on the hill with our names on it but what's true.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:5:The desk clerk opened the assessment wing on the First-day, as he had opened it every working morning for nine years. He hung up his coat. He took the cover off his ledger and sharpened a pencil, and he had the day book open and the date written before he looked round to say good morning to the copying table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:25:The stove in the corner was cold. He opened it, since he had opened everything else. It was full to the bars of fine pale ash, as soft as flour. It was a great deal of ash. He had been in the wing on the Sixth-day and the stove had been raked out then, for the bout. He could not think what a man would burn in one mild Seventh-day to make so much, and after a moment he stopped trying, because he found that he did not wish to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:31:The wing's weekly summaries hung on it in their rows, from the first week of the year before last to the week just ended, each of them one sheet in the same small square hand, pinned at its two top corners. The floor hours were there, and the cards, and the figures by cohort, and the corrections, with a margin wherever a correction had been made and the reason for it. The last sheet was dated the Seventh-day. It was finished, and totalled, and ruled off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:45:A man on the house's strength had not come to his work. He was not an enrollee, whose leaving had a form, and he was not faculty, whose leaving had a council. He had been placed with the wing two years ago by a district office's letter, as teaching assistants were, and it was to that office that the house now owed a question. Bracken wrote it before noon, in his upright hand, with one pair of glasses on, and he put no adjective in it, for he had none he could prove. It went down the hill in the First-day bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:51:This one was looked up. The name on it stood in a district's roll of placed staff, with a posting against it and a date. The clerk who found it there would ordinarily have answered within the week: *not recalled by this office; no instruction; the house may fill the place.* He began to write exactly that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:71:It was the right question, the one any careful officer asks when a quiet man goes. Nobody at that desk thought he had taken anything. He had sat at a copying table, and held four keys, and laid all four out in a row. But for two years he had been closer to one subject than anybody else in the service, and before that he had been close to sixty-two others, and he had watched, and written what he saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:95:It was a mild day for a fire, and Seln had lit one anyway, as soon as the desk clerk's stool stood empty for the Seventh-day. There were nineteen bundles in the cupboard under the copying table, standing on their ends in the office's grey tape, each one his own duplicates from a single posting. He had carried them from town to town for fifteen years and had never been able to say what for. A man who copied his mother's ledgers at a copper a page did not learn afterward to keep only one of a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:117:He did not go by the gate. The lodge-man kept a column for every bell, and Seln had read that column on a good many Seventh-days. He went by the stable passage and the farm track, as he had taken four people on a night in the recess, through an orchard that was black and smelled of wet bark. He walked down the middle of the track, where the grass was shortest, and at the gardener's gate he lifted the latch and let it down behind him without a click. It swung true; the large one had mended its top hinge with a mallet and a wedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:203:On a Seventh-day he wrote a return. He had not written this week's; he had been at a stove. But the pattern was fifteen years deep and did not know that it had been retired, and while his hands laid sticks it composed the thing by itself, in the voice he kept for the audit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:207:*Subject withdrew from the house on the Seventh-day, by the registrar's form, in good standing. Subject left the house at dusk with three associates and a cart. Subject's floor work is in the house's book for the Sixth-day, which this officer attended. Subject's associations unchanged. Nothing further to report.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:He read it over. Every word was true. It was the last of its kind, and it was as good as any of them; a reader could have checked each line against a book and found it stood, and he would have learned nothing about a boy who had let a coat fall in front of twelve hundred people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:3:Bracken wound the counter clock on the Seventh-day before the first bell, and nobody had ever been allowed to speak to him while he did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:"It stays true. A house does not certify a tier and cannot uncertify one, and that line is on the form only so that the registry may know whom it is reading about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:25:She took the pen and wrote the day, and her name, with the long tail on it that she had been told at Fenmark was not correct. Then she looked at the third line, which had a rule printed under it and, at the rule's end, in the registry's small type, *the enrollee may leave this line blank*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:45:"It is." Bracken had taken the glasses off his nose and was holding them. "I put it there on the evening I walked your docket up to a refectory table. A finding for advancement is signed before the registrar of the practitioner's house, and I thought that when yours came I should like the pen to have been waiting. It was a small vanity, and I have had it cleaned every Seventh-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:71:*Enrollee: Caelen Hesk-ward.* It was the name the registry had given him at Denvash, the one that stood on every instrument and that nobody who knew him had ever used, and Bracken had written it out in full, because a form was an instrument. *House: Halcenvane. Basis of enrollment: the demonstration provision of the assay clause; granted at Greyvane; twice renewed.* And on the line for tier, where Lira's leaf said *Iron* and Brom's said *Copper*, the registrar had written the only true thing there was to write, as he had seen it written on a door log at a wool exchange every Fifth-day for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:91:*Withdrawn at the registrar's counter, a quarter past the first bell of the Seventh-day, before any notice of date had been served upon this house; freely, upon the enrollee's own motion, and not by any officer of this institution.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:113:"It is," said Bracken. He had a second paper ready beside her leaf, and it ran to a full sheet. "An enrollee who holds standing under the research council does not only withdraw. She lapses, by heads, and the house is bound to read her the heads, so that nobody may say afterward that a thing was taken from her unawares. I drew it from the council's minute, and I believe it agrees with a sheet of your own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:171:"I am going to say something that has no place in procedure," he said. "I shall not enter it. You may, if you keep books."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:209:"One. I found another." It came out too fast. "A struck heading, in the index to the second volume, under *seats*. It's pencilled through like the first, and the section's still in the code behind it, and nobody's cited it since before the hall was built. I found it on the Fifth-day and I didn't bring it to you. I've written the note myself, because I wanted to see whether I could."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:247:The rotation's book was in his coat. It was a thin grey thing, cheaper than any other book he owned, and it held a year and a half of Fifth-days: what to take late, and what to give, and the figure each was meant to buy. Its last line said *All of it.* It was a true record. It was also the only paper on the hill that could turn a house found compliant into a house that had signed figures an enrollee arranged, and Gault's name stood under every one of those figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:249:He carried it down the corridor to the stove at the stair's head, which the porter kept in through the last week, for the damp. He opened the door and fed it the book a gathering at a time, and watched each one catch and go grey and fall in. It was the first record he had ever destroyed, and he thought that Karis would have understood it, and that Seln would not have needed to be told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:The district officer's envelope came back to the seat at Ostrand on the Seventh-day evening, by his own hand, opened and endorsed. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* The certificate followed in the house's bag on the First-day morning, with four leaves of the registry's own form under the house's seal, and one of the four had three lines of longhand in its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:9:He then had before him a ruling, given under the Compact's hand two days earlier, and an enrollment which that ruling revoked from the year's close; and the enrollment had ended of itself before the close arrived. He turned to the code. It had a word for a matter that has lost its subject, and he wrote the word in the proceedings' last column, small, and blotted it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:125:The gelding had spent the night under the pentice and was no more pleased with the morning than Cael was. Lira backed him between the shafts, talking, and did up the buckles in the order she had been shown. Karis had lifted a corner of the canvas before she so much as washed, and counted three boxes, and let the corner fall and tied it down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:269:"I was not there to see," said Seln. "I imagine it was noticed. I left the keys where they could be counted." He turned his cup a quarter turn on his knee. "I have filed nothing today. It is the first First-day in fifteen years, and I had expected to mind it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:47:"The rule is for water that covers a voice," he said. "And for water that things walk down to at night. That is a seep on a scree. It would not cover a cough, and nothing comes to it but us." He bent again. "A rule has its reasons written beside it so that one may tell when they are absent. I should stay where you are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:195:It was a Sixth-day. That was what the hill would have called it. But the hill's week had closed on the Fourth-day, behind a pillar with one blank face, and he did not think he had the use of its days any longer. Karis had settled the hour, and he supposed he might settle this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:197:*Sixth-day, by the hill's count. The second day past the Line, by ours.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:283:The one to Vell was shorter. She had kept a ledger that rated him before any registry did, and he had written to her at the close of each year. He told her that the year had closed, and that he had been found lawful and revoked in one paragraph. He wrote that he did not think her ledger had ever been wrong about him, and that he was now somewhere it could not be checked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:293:Nothing can be said of the room. It may be said that there was a table, and a lamp, since the work could not have been done without them. There was a clerk, and of the clerk it can be said only that the hand was practised, and that it had closed a great many files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:43:"Every second day," said Karis, breathing hard. She had stopped beside it with her wedge. "I said so, and I was guessing. It may be every tenth, up here. It may be when somebody remembers." She touched the bare hook with one pale finger. "I wonder who decided. Somebody sat at a desk and decided that past this post a letter wasn't worth a horse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:105:They left him at first light on the Fourth-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:137:The rider took out his book and wrote in it, not long. Cael did not need to see the line. *Subject passed the provincial bound at noon on the Fourth-day, eastward, with four others and a cart.* It would say four, and it would be the first paper anywhere to say it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:395:He sat on a rock above the water with a mule beside him, eating something out of a cloth, and he had two leather bags across the mule's back, flat, with long flaps. He looked at them across the stream without getting up. He had clearly been there some time, waiting for the water to fall, and was in no hurry about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:431:"Boxes," he said, to nobody, and shook his head. He rode down into the stream, which he had been waiting all morning to see fall, and went through it at a walk and up the far side, and he did not look back either. Nobody out here seemed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:61:"I'm going east with them, past the last station, and I won't sit the Silver; and this is the best floor anybody ever built me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:147:Cael looked at the table under the window. They lay where they had lain for two nights: on the left the district's finding, folded along its folds, with the printed slip tucked into it, and on the right, a hand's breadth off, the envelope from Velmere, with the red seal on it whole. There was a little dust on both already. Brom had cleared the summons and the dockets from under the whetstone and burned them in the corridor stove, and he had left these two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:215:"You'll be Hesk-ward," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:333:The woman who kept the guesting-house asked no questions. She had come up the bluff with a basket the day before, and she had seen what there was to see; she gave them the parlour with the two-paned door, and they put the cart in her yard, under the pentice, beside the old screw press.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:25:The stove in the corner was cold. He opened it, since he had opened everything else. It was full to the bars of fine pale ash, as soft as flour. It was a great deal of ash. He had been in the wing on the Sixth-day and the stove had been raked out then, for the bout. He could not think what a man would burn in one mild Seventh-day to make so much, and after a moment he stopped trying, because he found that he did not wish to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:57:Nothing can be said of that desk. It had no board beside its door, and its building was like the buildings on either side of it, and the person who sat at it was neither cruel nor kind in any way that the work required. There was a drawer, and in the drawer were the postings of a number of quiet people, and against each posting a bundle of what that person had sent in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:The name was found in the drawer. It had nineteen postings against it, in fifteen years, and the last was a chartered house on a bluff above a river. From that house a return had come up every week for two years, on the same day, folded in the same way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold. The desk turned to the last of them, which was dated eight days before the query.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:They checked the lines against station books, and house ledgers, and bout sheets, and each one stood. A subject had attended. A figure was as the house had entered it. An instrument had been declined at a gate, and the subject had not been seen at the refusal. Associations were unchanged. *Nothing further to report.* It was as clean a body of product as any reader there had handled, and one of them said so, and it was put in a minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:95:It was a mild day for a fire, and Seln had lit one anyway, as soon as the desk clerk's stool stood empty for the Seventh-day. There were nineteen bundles in the cupboard under the copying table, standing on their ends in the office's grey tape, each one his own duplicates from a single posting. He had carried them from town to town for fifteen years and had never been able to say what for. A man who copied his mother's ledgers at a copper a page did not learn afterward to keep only one of a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:99:He did not read them. There was nothing in them to read; he had seen to that when he wrote them. He untied each tape, and laid the leaves in by the handful, and waited until they had gone grey before he put in the next. A river town went first, where he had learned to fold a return. Then a guild hall, and a district seat in an old building, which he fed in rather quickly. There was an academy with four subjects, and a station in the hills with a green corridor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:107:He held it a little longer than the rest. It was two years of true weeks. *Subject attended. Subject's figure as entered by the house.* He had been proud of the others once, and there was a commendation for them in a drawer. He found that he was not proud of this one. He was something else, and he did not have the word, and the catechism had never supplied it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:131:He took the upper lane through the town, behind the exchange, where the windows were dark. In one of those streets four people were asleep, or lying awake, with a cart in a yard. Beyond the last houses the east road began, and the first of its stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:137:He had not known what he was copying, though the few who learned of it assumed otherwise. He had gone to that press for a subject's travel endorsements, which had been filed where they did not belong, and he had found paper that was correct in every particular and sat a little above where it should have sat; and he had copied it because he copied things. Understanding had come a year later, in a station corridor painted green, and it had come all at once, so that he had stood very still against a wall while a keeper went by with a tray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:153:He knew better. It was the first thing he had told them in the orchard, and he had made each of them say it back. You did not halt on a crest. You did not stand in the middle of a road with the sky behind you, where any child could count you. You went over the top without turning your head, and a hundred yards on, where the ground had dropped, you could look from behind something if you must.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:163:It was high up, at the top of the tallest roof, a small square of yellow, turned up as high as a wick would go. He had sat under that lamp on the evening of a birthday, at a table with a honey cake on it, and said that it was a good room. It had been kept burning every night since, so that the valley would see what it always saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:167:He stood and worked it through, since that was his trade. It would be the girl with ink on her collar. She would have asked nobody. She knew nothing, and had lit it on no instruction, and she would go on lighting it without knowing why. And every night it burned, the people at the road's foot would write that the room at the top of the library was occupied as usual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:215:He did not fold it in the old way. There was no outside fold to put a reference on, and no bag, and no drawer at the end of any road he meant to walk. He twisted the leaf into a spill instead, and put it under the smallest sticks, where the heap wanted something dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:The ninth part was drafted more widely. It allowed the registry to restrict, freeze or hold whatever attached to an unclassified person, pending the resolution of his status, and it did not wait upon a frame. The fences for it had been walked in the first term, and five drawers in five offices held their lists. The instrument was sealed, and went out by five bags.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:The house on the bluff answered first, in a registrar's upright hand. No enrollment stood, and no sponsorship; no floor hours were held, and no travel paper had been issued under the house's seal that had not lapsed with the form. The district seat answered that it kept nothing in that name but the file. The archive at Ternhall returned that the privileges in question had been surrendered at a counter, by their holder, before the instrument was dated. A provincial registry returned that the practitioner named as an associate stood at Copper, as he had for years; it added that a finding in his favour lay unsigned, and that the registry could not restrict a rank which nobody had taken up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:39:Two polite men had been through Denvash before. They had come in the first term to ask after an instrument-maker's trade licences and the title to his shop, and they had come again in the recess to see whether his answer had changed, and it had not. They came now a third time, with the sealed instrument in a wallet, and asked the old man at the bench whether his grandson had left any property on the premises.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:He was not anywhere. A watcher's line had him on the east road, four days out, with a cart, and there was no line after that. Beyond the last station no district ran, and a restriction must run in a district; it wanted a counter to be served at, and a book to be written in. The code had foreseen that, as it foresaw most things. The clerk found the phrase in a schedule and wrote it at the instrument's foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:53:He put it in a drawer. It would lie there, sealed and entire, for as long as it must, and on the day that any one of four names was written in a book inside a district, it would be taken out and served within the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Enrollment: closed by resignation; the house's certificate annexed. Proceedings: moot. Re-evaluation: not executed. Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:83:There were true things he might have set there. He considered each of them, as he considered everything, and with each he saw at once what would be done with it. He had written three true sentences about this practitioner before, in an advisory, as exactly as he could make them, and somebody had lifted them out and used them as a warrant. He had petitioned to be heard and been refused in two lines. He had written a finding by a candle and addressed it to nobody; it was in the lowest drawer, under a tab, and no proceeding had ever come that was worth sending it to. The one sentence of his that they still needed he had withheld, and a clerk had entered its absence on a form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:109:He drew the next thing toward him. It was a hill station's request for a second frame, and he read it through from its head to its seal, and dipped his pen, and answered it in full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:135:It had done so a dozen times since the yard gate. *Edge?* It asked about the cart's tailboard, where the oak was planed square, and about a milestone's arris, and the lip of a culvert, and the sharp fold in the canvas where the rope pulled it over a box. It was quicker than the other and less patient, and it held each thing up to him like a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:161:The keeper looked at the cart, and the canvas, and the four of them. A carter had a manifest, and it was stamped at every water and every waystation as far as the stamps ran; Lira had sat on a landing wall and learned that from three people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:181:A man had come round the bend of the road behind them, on a brown cob, perhaps three hundred yards back. He wore a grey travelling coat, and he was not hiding. He rode at a walk in the middle of the road, with his reins slack, and when the cart stopped he stopped as well and let the horse put its head down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:197:They passed a waystation at midday and did not stop at it. It was the one with the well. The carter with the good horses had told Lira about that well, and Lira had told the rest of them twice, and Karis had written it down. There was a chartered sign over its door with the registry's seal burned into the wood, and under the sign a board of tolls and hours, and a boy sat on the trough and watched them go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:231:It was built, and lit, and burning, on a level place under the brake's lee, beside a flat stone, and somebody had laid it small to large. A heap of broken ash stood by it, stacked, with the thick ends one way, and there was a can of water on the stone. There were five places round the fire where the grass had been trodden flat, and Cael looked at those for longer than he looked at anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:13:It asked him about that edge. It had been quiet since the station, the quick one, and he had thought it was done. But there had never been an edge like this, which ran for four hundred yards with a hundred feet of nothing on the far side of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:33:There was nothing on it. Cael saw that as he stood with the stakes in his hand. Every place they had slept that week had been made by somebody: a woman had kept a parlour, and a narrow man a long room, and an old man had cut turf; even at the last station there had been a roof, hung on a carter's wire, and at the crossroads a fire had been waiting. This was a shelf on a hill, with a pine tree on it. Whatever it was going to be by dark, they would have to make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:51:He put a light to the fire as the sun went off the shelf, and it caught at once. The dead pine was full of resin, and it burned with a clear high flame and a smell Cael had never met, and he stood and watched it take.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:53:It was only a fire. But no keeper had laid it, and it stood on no map; it burned on the second day past the Line, above a valley that had no name on any sheet, and no Arbiter had ever measured the ground it stood on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:61:"Lira slept through at the crossroads," she said. "Brom at the first waystation, though nobody watched there. I did, on the hill, and Cael at the station. Last night nobody slept at all, under that cart, so I haven't counted it. It's you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:105:On a hill a week behind them, a man with his arm in a sling would be walking a cohort of first-years round a yard, and telling them that the rota was a joke. He would have a board with his name on it. It was the right place for him, and he had said so himself before anybody could say it for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:137:Everything was on it. The Ost was there, and the river towns, and Auremont and Norhold and Greyvane had their names in small capitals. Every district had its bound in a fine line and every station a mark like a seed, and Denvash was at the far left edge, so small that he had to look twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:139:He found Ostrand, and put his finger on it and went east. There was the first ford, and the second, and a hill drawn as a caterpillar, and a waystation, and beyond it a station. A little to the right of the station there was a ruled line, top to bottom, straight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:143:"That's where we are," said Karis. She put one pale finger into the box with the key in it. "Under *chartered waystation*, I think. Or a little above the seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:"That," said Karis, "is the difficulty with the figure. I can tell you what it buys at a waystation. I've no price list for here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:249:The fire had burned down a little. He put on a limb of the pine, and it flared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:261:*I can work within the system. I have the receipts: a hearing, a provision, a stamp renewed four times, a tournament their own clause put me in. The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed. This is more text. We'll read it better than they do. We always have.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:317:*Status: active non-compliance, observation priority escalated.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:329:He had not yet lain down. He was sitting by the fire with his coat round him and his cup in his hands, and Lira and Karis were awake as well, since nobody had found the way to sleep on the first night of a place. Brom had come back from the lip to warm his hands. So all five of them were there, and the pine was burning with its high clear flame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:361:The fire burned. Below the shelf a valley lay in the dark, with water going through it, and nobody had ever told him what it was called; beyond it there were seven ridges that he had counted, and more that he had not. Brom went back to the lip and sat down facing outward. After a little while Seln lay down under the pine, on his side, with the satchel under his head, and shut his eyes as a man does who has decided to trust a table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:7:They slept the second night at a waystation with a long room and a stove. Its keeper was a narrow man who counted out hay for the gelding and the cob behind them, and then named a figure, and Brom went out to the rack with a lantern and came back and laid down exactly half of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:13:"The other beast's rider pays for his own. I check." Brom did not raise his voice. He went back out to the yard with the lantern, and he was gone some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:41:The metalling gave out first, and he marked the place: a last yard of broken stone rammed flat, and after it the road was earth, rutted, with grass down its middle. The ditches ended a little higher. Then the milestones changed. *Forty-one* was dressed, and had the river and the scale cut over its number; *forty-two* was a rough upright of the hill's own rock, with a figure daubed on it in white; and there was no forty-three, though he looked for it for an hour. At a place where a track came in there was an iron post with a hook on it, and nothing on the hook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:49:Behind them, three hundred yards down the slope, the grey rider had got off his cob and was leading it. He kept his distance on foot exactly as he had kept it mounted, and when they stopped, he stopped, and let the horse crop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:55:It was the last chartered waystation, and it had the sign to prove it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:The board hung from an iron arm over the door, with the registry's seal burned into it, and the wind had swung it against the wall so often that it had worn a pale groove in the stone. There was a yard with a wall round it, and a well with a lid, and a stack of cut turf against the gable, higher than a man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:75:Cael stopped in front of it, and Karis was there before him. It was the registry's own print, under glass, with the seal at its head, and it was the last such board on that road. They had passed one at every waystation since Ostrand, and he had read each of them in passing, as he read everything; he read this one through, standing, with his arm aching in its strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:77:[[TIER-B: the waystation board — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:83:"Who stamps the carters' sheets?" said Lira. "They told me the stamps ran as far as the station. That's a day on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:85:"They run as far as my drawer." He nodded at the table. "There's been no keeper at the station these four years. They took the man and left me his brass, and a letter to say I was to mark for him until further instruction, and I've had no further instruction. So a sheet says *station*, and it means an old man at a turf fire a day short of it." He looked into the fire. "Nobody's complained. There's nobody past here to complain to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:Seln had not come to the board. He stood inside the door with his satchel on him, and he was reading the room, not the wall; Cael saw his eyes go to the drawer in the table, and stay there a breath, and move on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:91:It was not under glass. It had been pinned up bare, a long time ago, and had gone brown at its edges. Cael knew the print, and he knew the sheet, for it was the registry's public notice of tiers, which he had seen on the wall of every station and guild hall he had ever stood in, and in an instrument-maker's shop, behind the door. There were five words down its left side in heavy type, Copper, Iron, Bronze, Silver and Gold, and against each stood its ranks, and the fee for an evaluation, and the office to which a practitioner should apply.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:133:The grey rider had come up closer than he had been in four days, perhaps eighty yards off. He was mounted again, and he rode up the last of the rise at a walk, and stopped at the pillar's foot on its western side, with the cob's nose level with the stone. He did not come past it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:167:The station stood on the far side, a little above the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:169:He knew it by its shape, as a carter had known it: a low stone house with a slate roof, and behind it a second building with no windows and a wide door. There was a bench under the wall. A board for the day's hours hung beside the door, and the rain had washed it clean a long time ago. Over the door, in the lintel, a sigil was cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:191:It was the station's register. Cael came and looked over her shoulder. It was ruled in columns, in a printed hand: the day, the name, the age, the reading. There was a narrow column beside the reading, with no heading printed on it, where a keeper might note what else his frame had shown, and the last column was headed *Entered*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:"It stops," she said. "Four years ago. Here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:215:Cael looked, and it took him a moment. The walls of the station were the grey rock of the upland, split and laid in courses, and the lintel was grey too. But the sigil was not cut in the lintel. It was cut in a separate block, darker, closer in the grain, a foot square, and the lintel had been notched to take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:219:"The wall's built round it." Karis had come close, and was looking at the joint. "The courses are cut to fit; you can see where the mason trimmed them. So the stone was here first, and they brought the house to it." She turned to the counter, through the open door. "The register has a first leaf. It says the station was established *at the sigil*. I took that for a manner of speaking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:231:They slept in the station. It was the first roof that week which nobody charged them for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:279:"It's a morning's walk," said Karis. "That's the wording. *The bound of a station is a morning's walk from its sigil.* A keeper walked it once, I suppose, and came home for his dinner. Nobody ever wrote down where he turned."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:319:He walked back to where Seln stood, and looked west, with his arms hanging, as he looked at a rack of plates. The track ran back across the upland, pale, as far as the roofs of the station, with the water beyond, and the bare rise. There was no rider on it. There was no lantern, and no tin cup, and no man with a pencil on a top tier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:353:It came out of the west, behind them, and it stayed. By the middle of the afternoon the track was running, and by dusk they could not see a hundred yards. There was no thorn up there, and no wall. Brom chocked the cart on the lee side of a low bank and they ran the canvas out from its bed on two of the gelding's hobble-stakes, and all five of them got in under it, with the boxes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:355:There was no fire, for there was nothing on that upland to burn that was not soaked. They ate what the last keeper had sold them, cold. Seln named the watches, two and two, and Brom took the first with Lira, and the dusk came down grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:357:The quiet thing stirred in it, as it always did at that hour. Cael felt it lift, soft, behind everything else he carried, and he held it where it was. It had been sealed from anybody who could carry the seeing west. He looked out under the canvas's edge at a hundred yards of rain, and thought that there was now nobody within three days who could carry anything anywhere, and that the seal's own terms had very nearly run out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:423:"It's a copper as far as the waystation. He'll mark it there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:473:It was all he could carry. He went up behind the wheel with a block of ash in his sound hand, and each time the gelding stopped he put it down. He had stood on a stone floor against a Gold, in front of eight thousand people, and he had gone down the Crown yard on a river of dust; and he climbed the first ridge east of the Line stopping a cart from rolling backward, and it was the most useful thing he did that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:The pen lay where Lira had left it. It was a plain steel pen in a dark holder, with the house's mark burned into the wood, and it had lain at that end of the counter for longer than Cael had troubled to notice. Bracken had said so on a landing two nights ago. *There is a pen on my counter. There has been one there since the fourth day of the year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:71:*Enrollee: Caelen Hesk-ward.* It was the name the registry had given him at Denvash, the one that stood on every instrument and that nobody who knew him had ever used, and Bracken had written it out in full, because a form was an instrument. *House: Halcenvane. Basis of enrollment: the demonstration provision of the assay clause; granted at Greyvane; twice renewed.* And on the line for tier, where Lira's leaf said *Iron* and Brom's said *Copper*, the registrar had written the only true thing there was to write, as he had seen it written on a door log at a wool exchange every Fifth-day for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:81:Cael wrote the day. He had held nothing but a pencil since the rim of the Crown yard, and his left arm lay along the counter in its binding, stiff from the wrist to the elbow, and steadied the leaf by its weight alone. He wrote the name as the leaf had it, all of it, in the careful hand he kept for things other people would read, and it looked like a stranger's; he supposed that was correct too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:241:The satchel took the rest, as it had been cut to. Hesk's book went into its pocket, and behind it the old volume, full to its last leaf, with the letters under its front board, and beside them the observation notebook, which held two years of the Crown yard's east tier. He did not own a thing he would grieve to lose that was not now on that strap.

