     1	# Chapter 58 — Pending Jurisdiction
     2	
     3	The machine took a week over it, and did nothing that was not correct.
     4	
     5	The district officer's envelope came back to the seat at Ostrand on the Seventh-day evening, by his own hand, opened and endorsed. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* The certificate followed in the house's bag on the First-day morning, with four leaves of the registry's own form under the house's seal, and one of the four had three lines of longhand in its margin.
     6	
     7	The seat's clerk read the margin twice. He had no heading for a margin, and he entered the leaf as received.
     8	
     9	He then had before him a ruling, given under the Compact's hand two days earlier, and an enrollment which that ruling revoked from the year's close; and the enrollment had ended of itself before the close arrived. He turned to the code. It had a word for a matter that has lost its subject, and he wrote the word in the proceedings' last column, small, and blotted it.
    10	
    11	*Moot.*
    12	
    13	It did not undo the ruling, for nothing undid a ruling. It meant only that the paper would lie in its press, complete and lawful, with nobody left under it.
    14	
    15	There remained the order for re-evaluation, and that was not the clerk's to close. It had a date upon it, and a room.
    16	
    17	So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
    18	
    19	The order was called, and the name was read as the instruments had it.
    20	
